.\" karvi-askpass(1): the askpass helper's manual page, written by hand; the
.\" helper takes no flags, so no region is generated. The behaviour it
.\" states is internal/askpass's.
.TH KARVI-ASKPASS 1 "" "karvi"
.\" Paths and names are not hyphenated, and lines are not stretched.
.nh
.ds AD l
.ad l
.SH NAME
karvi\-askpass \- deliver one credential field from karvi to OpenSSH
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B karvi\-askpass
.RI [ PROMPT ]
.PP
.BR ssh (1)
runs it for karvi; an operator does not.
.SH DESCRIPTION
When karvi opens a device session through its system transport, it starts
.B ssh
with
.B SSH_ASKPASS
naming this helper and
.B SSH_ASKPASS_REQUIRE=force
in the environment, and
.B ssh
runs the helper with its prompt as the one argument whenever it needs an
answer.
The helper sends the one\-use token from
.B KARVI_ASKPASS_TOKEN
and the prompt to the broker karvi holds open at the socket
.BR KARVI_ASKPASS_SOCKET ,
and writes the one answer and a newline to standard output, which
.B ssh
reads.
.PP
The broker classifies the prompt as a username, a password, an enable
password, or a host\-key question, and answers with the credential field
karvi resolved for the session, or with
.B yes
to the host\-key question, karvi's host\-key policy having decided it
already.
Any other prompt is refused.
.PP
Run by hand, without the two variables, the helper prints nothing and
exits 2.
.PP
karvi finds the helper at
.B KARVI_ASKPASS_PATH
when that names a regular file, else beside the
.B karvi
executable
.RB ( karvi\-askpass ,
or
.B karvi\-askpass\-linux\-amd64
in a release tree), else on
.BR PATH .
.SH ENVIRONMENT
.TP
.BR KARVI_ASKPASS_SOCKET ", " KARVI_ASKPASS_TOKEN
The broker's socket and the session's one\-use token; karvi sets them for
the one
.B ssh
process.
.TP
.B KARVI_ASKPASS_PATH
The helper's path; karvi reads it, and the daemon's launcher passes it
through the environment filter that keeps an operator's shell secrets out
of the daemon.
.SH SECURITY
The token is 32 random bytes, compared in constant time.
The socket is mode 0600 in the operator's scratch directory.
The broker accepts one connection per session, then closes and removes
the socket; it waits at most five minutes for that connection, and the
exchange is bounded to ten seconds.
Both sides wipe their copy of the secret.
The broker refuses with
.B askpass_auth_failed
(a wrong token),
.B askpass_request_invalid
(a request it cannot decode),
.B askpass_prompt_unrecognized
(a prompt it does not classify), and
.BR askpass_username_unset ,
.BR askpass_password_unset ,
or
.B askpass_enable_password_unset
(a field the session did not resolve).
.SH EXIT STATUS
.TP
.B 0
The answer was written.
.TP
.B 2
The socket or the token is not set.
.TP
.B 3
The socket was not reached within five seconds.
.TP
.B 4
The request was not sent.
.TP
.B 5
No answer came, or the broker refused.
.TP
.B 6
The answer was not written.
.SH FILES
.TP
.I askpass\-*.sock
The broker's socket, in the scratch directory
.RB ( tempdir ;
.I /dev/shm/karvi/USER
first in its
.B auto
chain).
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR karvi\-prune (8),
.BR ssh (1);
.I docs/OPERATIONS.md
"Daemon environment" and
.I docs/ARCHITECTURE.md
in the karvi source.
